*** If too slow, see section on "Dictionary" below

Intended improvements: Working with menu systems
Or macros to impliment an alternative menu system

Installation with Macro System
==============================

1.  Copy all WVE and DAT files to M:\SPK directory
2.  Make sure M:\WVE directory exists
3.  Copy all OPLs to M:\OPL directory and translate to M:\OPO directory
4.  Install KMac macro system to Psion as normal
5.  Delete default KMac.Kma and put Speech.Kma in that directory to use
speech when launching KMac
6.  When KMac is launched, pressing Ctrl-Shift-S speaks selected text:
- If there is any highlighted text, speaks highlighted text
- Otherwise, speaks currently selected entry in Agenda, or current cell in
spreadsheet
- Notice that it works identically to the Bring option

***

As it stands, the program accepts input from the keyboard and builds, plays
and deletes a WVE file - so have enough room.  The *.wve files should be on
LOC::m:\spk\, as should SPEECH.DAT, and SPEECH.OPL is the program to run.
SORTFILE.OPL should be run if you add to SPEECH.DAT, producing the new
SPEECH2.DAT which you could rename SPEECH.DAT.

SPEECH.DAT is the dictionary.  The program uses it until it either finds a
match or drops a character from the input.  It translates into the filenames
of the .WVE files.  For example, if "superior*ssooppeerriiorrr" is a line in
the dictionary, it would play SS.WVE followed by OO.wve followed by PP.wve
and so on if it finds "superior" in the input string.  The dictionary also
contains part words.

The # character may be used once in both input and output strings (either side
of the *).  It stands for any letter, and is replaced by double that letter.
So "i#y*iy#ee" replaces "ity" with "iyttee".  If the last character of the
input is an underline, the translation is only accepted if the next character
is a space or punctuation.  Spaces may also be included in the input.  Please
keep the input lower case.

Don't worry if you try to play some of the .WVE files using Record and get a
"No sound to play" message - Record only bothers if the files are above a
certain length, and many of them are not.

The program could be made faster with a shorter dictionary - try deleting some
of the longer entries toward the beginning - but it is then less accurate and
spelling has to be altered more often in order to get correct pronunciation.

If transferring the files using Odyssey's YModem protocol, use YModem-G.  It
is more likely to work.

Using speech in your own OPL programs - a quick guide
=====================================================
Use the instruction:
LOADM "SPEECH"
early on in the program.  Then, instead of PRINT "Hello", use
say:("Hello")
Etc.
